//! **How wide, and how tall, the desktop's text is** (WASM-016 R14): the stand-in face's metrics, and the
//! arithmetic the desktop's text layout does with them.
//!
//! The desktop draws "Arial" in Ubuntu Light (`assets/web-fonts`), laid out by egui. This module is that
//! layout's arithmetic with no egui in it, so the Web player — which has no egui — answers the same
//! questions with the same numbers, and a test in this crate (`tests/text_metrics_follow_egui.rs`,
//! `render` feature) holds the two together by laying the same text out in egui and comparing:
//!
//! * a line is the sum of its glyphs' advances at the font size and the face's pair kerning between them;
//! * a font's ascent, descent and line gap are each scaled to the size and rounded to 1/32 of a point
//!   (`GUI_ROUNDING`); a row is as tall as their sum **rounded to a whole pixel**;
//! * the baseline sits `round(ascent + row - sum)` below the top of the row — the glyphs are bottom-aligned
//!   in the row, so the pixels a row gains by rounding up go above them;
//! * a galley is put at `round(top)`, and a glyph's baseline is that plus the baseline offset above.
//!
//! Where a surface has to decide something from the width of a text (a caption that is shrunk until it
//! fits, a legend whose entries are centred as one group) or from where its baseline falls, it asks
//! here, so the answer is the desktop's and not the browser's.
//!
//! The advances below are the font's own `hmtx` values for Basic Latin, Latin-1, Latin Extended-A and the
//! punctuation a form's captions use (329 glyphs); a character the face does not hold falls to the next
//! face of the desktop's chain, which this table does not carry, so it is counted at half an em.

/// The face's `unitsPerEm`.
pub const UNITS_PER_EM: f64 = 1000.0;
